{# HOMEPAGE FAQ                                              #}
{# ======================================================== #}
<section class="homepage-faq">
  <div class="wrap homepage-faq__inner">
    <div class="homepage-faq__header">
      <p class="eyebrow">The Case File</p>
      <h2>What is Brooke Reports?</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="homepage-faq__list">
      <article class="homepage-faq__item">
        <h3>What is Brooke Reports?</h3>
        <p>Brooke Reports is a cozy field guide to persuasion literacy, marketing psychology, attention, and clear thinking. It helps readers understand how messages, platforms, offers, social pressure, and emotional framing influence decisions.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="homepage-faq__item">
        <h3>Who is Brooke Reports for?</h3>
        <p>Brooke Reports is for curious, ambitious people who want to make better decisions without becoming cynical. It's especially useful for course buyers, creators, freelancers, small business owners, online learners, and anyone who wants to understand how persuasion works in everyday life.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="homepage-faq__item">
        <h3>What does Brooke Reports cover?</h3>
        <p>Brooke Reports covers marketing psychology, sales psychology, media literacy, the attention economy, decision-making, cognitive biases, influence tactics, online offers, vague promises, urgency, social proof, and the hidden pressure inside modern communication.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="homepage-faq__item">
        <h3>Is Brooke Reports anti-marketing?</h3>
        <p>No. Brooke Reports is *not* anti-marketing. It *is* anti-confusion, anti-hype, and anti-pressure. Ethical persuasion helps people make informed choices. Manipulative persuasion tries to bypass judgment.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

